# evidence-for: FR-021, NFR-003
# evidence-timeout: 120

# D-188 -- migrate-archive.py DESTROYED this run's own server-written
# stream-rollup.json, because the C-6 cycle facts the spec's Data Model added
# made a CURRENT document look like the inert v1 document the rebuild exists to
# replace. `/foundry:resume` runs this tool on every resume, so the loss was one
# resume away from any run under this release. The compatibility regime it broke
# is A-044's, quoted in this casting's Informational block: "new fields are
# additive, migrate-archive.py learns them".
#
# THE OBSERVED LOSS, before the fix, driven on a byte copy of
# foundry-archive/daring-orca: step stream_rollup reported outcome "upgraded"
# and re-derived the document from markers. 45 keys went, including EVERY
# inspect_mode / inspect_rule / stream_scope / evidence_sweep entry for cycles
# 10, 11 and 12 -- the per-cycle record GI-008 requires the transition to write
# -- plus whole stream entries in eleven of thirteen cycles. Cycle 11's PROVE
# went from {items_checked 172, items_total 172, findings 2, records [one audit
# row]} to {items_checked 0, items_total 0, findings 2, records []}.
#
# THE THREE THINGS THIS LOG SHOWS.
#
# 1. c6_rollup -- a committed fixture archive written by THIS release, whose
#    roll-up carries the C-6 cycle facts (inspect_mode, inspect_rule,
#    stream_scope, evidence_sweep and the fifth key, temper_entry) BESIDE the
#    stream tranches. Every step no-ops and `migrated` is False: NFR-003
#    idempotence now holds against a current archive, which is the case it never
#    covered. Before the fix this same fixture reported "upgraded".
#
# 2. pre_change -- the pre-release fixture with no roll-up at all. Still
#    "created", derived from the archive's own defects and markers. The fix
#    narrows what counts as evidence of an OLD document; it does not stop the
#    tool doing its job on one. thunder-viper takes this same branch.
#
# 3. The predicate itself, over four document shapes. A genuine v1 document
#    (int-valued entries under canonical stream ids) still rebuilds. The other
#    three do not: a widened current document; a MIXED document -- a v1-migrated
#    archive resumed under this release, whose old cycles are inert ints while
#    its new ones carry the server's audit trail -- and a bucket holding only
#    cycle-level facts. Preserving dominates, because re-deriving a mixed
#    document destroys the half that cannot be reconstructed in order to repair
#    the half that can, and _rollup_needs_rebuild's own docstring has always
#    called that data loss.
#
# The value test is scoped by resolving the KEY against the stream roster first,
# then testing the value with the one definition in
# foundry_state.is_stream_record -- exactly measure-run.py's structure since
# D-182. A denylist of the four key NAMES the defect quoted was rejected:
# temper_entry is already a fifth, and the next C-6 field would be a sixth.
#
# Regression tests: tests/test_migrate_archive.py --
# test_step_4_never_rebuilds_over_c6_cycle_facts,
# test_a_c6_archive_migrates_as_a_whole_no_op,
# test_step_4_preserves_a_mixed_document_rather_than_half_rebuilding_it,
# test_step_6_reads_a_c6_rollup_step_4_left_as_found. All four fail at 0e09b40.

--- fixture: c6_rollup
    migrated: False
    stream_rollup: {"outcome": "no-op", "reason": "stream-rollup.json carries data no rebuild could reconstruct"}
--- fixture: pre_change
    migrated: True
    stream_rollup: {"outcome": "created", "cycles": 4, "streams": 3, "findings_per_stream": {"prove": 3, "test01": 1, "trace": 3}, "non_stream_sources": {"legacy_stream": 1}}
--- _rollup_needs_rebuild over four document shapes
    needs_rebuild=True   v1 int-valued, inert
    needs_rebuild=False  C-6 facts beside tranches
    needs_rebuild=False  mixed: v1 cycle + server-written cycle
    needs_rebuild=False  cycle-level facts only
